@page "/toolings/minifier"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="toolings/minifier"
            Title="bit Minifier - Toolings"
            Description="bit Minifier takes around 400 KB off what a Blazor WebAssembly app downloads, with no code changes, no settings and no runtime cost. Install it and publish in Release." />

<div class="page-container">
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H3" Element="h1" Gutter>bit Minifier</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>A smaller Blazor WebAssembly app, for free</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.Subtitle1" Gutter>
        Every kilobyte your users download is time they wait before your app starts.
        bit Minifier takes around 400 KB off what a Blazor WebAssembly app downloads, and asks nothing in return:
        no code changes, no settings and no cost at runtime. Install it in the Blazor WebAssembly project and publish in Release.
    </BitText>
    <CodeBox>dotnet add package Bit.Minifier</CodeBox>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Why it costs nothing</BitText>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Nothing to set up.</b> Install the package and publish in Release.</li>
                <li><b>Nothing at runtime.</b> The app runs the same code, only with shorter names and without the attributes nothing reads while it runs.</li>
                <li><b>Nothing during development.</b> <code>dotnet build</code>, <code>dotnet run</code> and <code>dotnet watch</code> are never touched; only a Release publish is.</li>
                <li><b>Nothing ships broken.</b> Nothing is written unless every reference still resolves. An assembly that can't be minified safely is left as it was, and the publish says which one and why.</li>
                <li><b>Still debuggable.</b> The pdbs are rewritten too, so breakpoints and line numbers keep working, and a map turns any stack trace back into the names your source has.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Numbers</BitText>
            The bit BlazorUI demo, its WebAssembly client published in Release, measured as browsers download it, brotli compressed:
            <div class="table-container">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th></th><th class="num">Download</th><th class="num">Saved</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Without it</td><td class="num">6,604 KB</td><td class="num"></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Default</td><td class="num">6,132 KB</td><td class="num">472 KB</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Aggressive</td><td class="num">6,064 KB</td><td class="num">540 KB</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <br />
            That's 7.2% and 8.2% less to download, for about 8 seconds more of publishing.
            The libraries give up the most: <code>Bit.BlazorUI</code> 594 KB of its 3.6 MB (-16%), <code>Newtonsoft.Json</code> 137 KB (-20%),
            <code>System.Text.Json</code> 72 KB (-18%). The app's own code keeps every name it has and still gives up 310 KB, all of it attributes.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>What it does</BitText>
            Trimming removes the code an app doesn't use. Right after it, bit Minifier rewrites every trimmed assembly, the app's, the libraries'
            and the framework's, along with its pdb. Every name that isn't public gets a short one, and the attributes only the compiler, debuggers
            and analyzers read (<code>[CompilerGenerated]</code>, <code>[Nullable]</code>, <code>[DebuggerDisplay]</code>, ...) go.
            <br />
            <br />
            What something reads by name at runtime stays:
            <ul>
                <li>names a string literal mentions, as <code>nameof(...)</code>, <code>GetMethod("...")</code> and <code>[UnsafeAccessor(Name = "...")]</code> do;</li>
                <li>public properties and fields, which serializers read;</li>
                <li>Blazor component types, and members with runtime attributes such as <code>[Parameter]</code> and <code>[JSInvokable]</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <b>Your own code keeps its names.</b> The assemblies of your own projects keep every name they have, so an exception your app logs
            reads as your source does. Only their attributes go.
            <br />
            <br />
            To turn it all off without uninstalling anything:
            <CodeBox>&lt;BitMinify>false&lt;/BitMinify></CodeBox>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Aggressive (experimental)</BitText>
            <CodeBox>&lt;BitMinifyAggressive>true&lt;/BitMinifyAggressive></CodeBox>
            Public names go too: public types and their namespaces, public non-virtual methods, fields and parameter names.
            What the runtime, a serializer or a server finds by name still stays, such as names a string mentions, virtual and interface members,
            properties, enum members and exception types.
            It's for apps whose names nothing reads from outside the WebAssembly client, published by someone who tests the result.
            Don't use it when code builds a name at runtime, a serializer reads public fields or type names, configuration names framework types,
            or a type name is stored across releases.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Reading a stack trace</BitText>
            Every publish writes a map of the names it changed to <code>obj/&lt;configuration>/&lt;tfm>/bit-minifier.map</code>.
            The <a href="@Urls.Cli">bit CLI</a> reads a stack trace your app logged back into the names its source has, without installing anything:
            <CodeBox>dnx Bit.Cli --version 10.7.0 -- decode bit-minifier.map trace.txt</CodeBox>
            turns
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>   at _aN+_a.MoveNext()
   at Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection._aD.ah[a,b](IServiceCollection services)</CodeBox>
            into
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>   at ...Services.HttpMessageHandlers.ExceptionDelegatingHandler+&lt;SendAsync>d__1.MoveNext()
   at Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.IServiceCollectionExtensions.AddSessioned[TService,TImplementation](IServiceCollection services)</CodeBox>
            Every publish renames afresh, so keep each release's map with the release, not next to the app.
            <code>BitMinifyMapOutputPath</code> copies it out of <code>obj</code> for a CI job to collect:
            <CodeBox>dotnet publish MyApp.Client.csproj -c Release -p:BitMinifyMapOutputPath=bit-minifier.map</CodeBox>
            The full reference is in the <a href="https://github.com/bitfoundation/bitplatform/tree/develop/src/Toolings/Minifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bit Minifier's README</a>.
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<NavigationButtons Prev="bit CLI" PrevUrl="@Urls.Cli" Next="BlazorES2019" NextUrl="@Urls.BlazorES2019" />
